# Card Tokenization JSON Schema Validator

## Background
Our tokenization service receives raw card data payloads from merchants before we vault them. We need a robust Python validator that ensures every incoming payload strictly conforms to PCI DSS requirements before we process it.

## Payload Schema

All incoming tokenization requests must have this top-level structure:

```json
{
  "request_id": "<uuid-v4>",
  "merchant": {
    "id": "<string, 8-20 alphanumeric chars>",
    "name": "<string, 1-200 chars>",
    "mcc": "<string, exactly 4 digits>"
  },
  "card": {
    "number": "<string, valid Luhn, 13-19 digits>",
    "expiry": {
      "month": "<integer, 1-12>",
      "year": "<integer, current year or later, max current+10>"
    },
    "cvv": "<string, 3-4 digits>",
    "holder_name": "<string, 2-100 chars, letters/spaces/hyphens/apostrophes only>"
  },
  "billing_address": {
    "line1": "<string, 1-100 chars>",
    "line2": "<string, optional, max 100 chars>",
    "city": "<string, 1-50 chars>",
    "state": "<string, 2-char ISO code>",
    "postal_code": "<string, 5-digit or 5+4 US zip, or 6-char alphanumeric for international>",
    "country": "<string, 2-char ISO 3166-1 alpha-2>"
  },
  "metadata": {
    "source": "<string: 'web' | 'mobile' | 'api'>",
    "ip_address": "<string, valid IPv4 or IPv6>",
    "user_agent": "<string, optional, max 512 chars>"
  }
}
```

